COMMAND NAME: mpprecoverseg.sh

Recovers a primary or mirror segment instance that has been marked 
as invalid (if mirroring is enabled).

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mpprecoverseg.sh [-d <data_directory>] [-f] [-z <seg_data_dir:seg_hostname>]
[-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>]

mpprecoverseg.sh [-? | -v]

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mpprecoverseg.sh script recovers a failed segment instance if 
you have mirroring enabled. A segment instance can fail for several 
reasons, such as the host goes down, the segment instance process is 
killed, or the data directory gets corrupted. When a segment instance 
goes down it is marked as invalid in the Greenplum Database global system 
catalog. In order to bring the segment instance back into operation 
again, you must first correct the problem that made it fail in the 
first place, and then recover the segment instance in the 
Greenplum Database system.

Greenplum Database can run in two modes with regards to mirroring and fault 
tolerance  continue (read-write) mode and read-only mode. This mode is 
set with the mpp_fault_action parameter in postgresql.conf at startup.

If the system is in continue (read-write) mode, this script will shutdown 
the system, copy the data directory of the active segment to the failed 
segment, update the segment dbid and port on the failed segment after the 
copy, mark the failed segment as valid again in the global system catalog, 
and restart the system.

If the system is in read-only mode, the script will recover the segment 
without shutting down the system. Since the data is read-only, the active 
segment and failed segment should not get out-of-sync in the event of a 
failure.

If your system is in shutdown mode, or if you do not have mirroring enabled, 
you must take manual steps to recover a failed segment instance. To manually 
mark a segment instance as valid again, run the following function on 
the master:

mpp.mpp_set_segdb_state(<dbid>, true|false)

Where <dbid> is the unique ID for a segment instance. True marks the 
segment as valid and false marks it as invalid.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the value 
set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.

-f

Optional. Force shutdown and startup of segment instances marked as invalid.


-z <seg_data_dir:seg_hostname>

Forces a recovery of a primary segment instance from its mirror even though 
the segment has not been marked invalid. Use this option with care.

-a

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.

-q

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, 
but is still written to the log file.

-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to <current_directory>/logs.

-?

Displays the online help.

-v

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Recover and failed segment instances:

./mpprecoverseg.sh

